\begin{proof}
Pair each element $g\in G$ with its inverse $g^{-1}$. If $g\neq g^{-1}$, these two elements form a distinct pair. The remaining elements are precisely those satisfying $g=g^{-1}$, equivalently $g^2=e$. Since $|G|$ is even, the number of remaining elements has the same parity as $|G|$, because all distinct inverse-pairs contribute an even number of elements. Thus the number of elements satisfying $g^2=e$ is even. Since $e$ is one such element, there is another element $a\neq e$ with $a^2=e$. Therefore $a$ has order $2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Partition $G$ into inverse-orbits. Each orbit has either two elements, of the form $\{g,g^{-1}\}$ with $g\neq g^{-1}$, or one element, consisting of a solution to $g=g^{-1}$, equivalently $g^2=e$. Since $G$ has even cardinality, the number of one-element inverse-orbits is even: the two-element orbits contribute an even number of elements. The identity is one of these one-element orbits. Therefore there is a second one-element orbit, represented by some $a\neq e$. Then $a=a^{-1}$, so $a^2=e$. Since $a$ is not the identity, its order is exactly $2$.
\end{proof}
